window.FIXTURES=window.FIXTURES||{};window.FIXTURES["speech"]={"vid":"suR3k5gYq4k","speaker":"Sadyr Zhaparov","role":"President","country":"Kyrgyzstan","region":"Asia-Pacific","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=suR3k5gYq4k","date":"20260922","title":"🇰🇬 Kyrgyzstan - President Addresses United Nations General Debate, 81st Session | #UNGA","seconds":1307,"words":2778,"counts":{"claims":41,"promises":7,"attacks":0},"topics":[{"topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","share":0.106,"seconds":139},{"topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","share":0.1038,"seconds":136},{"topic":"UNSC membership","share":0.0998,"seconds":130},{"topic":"UN sanctions regime","share":0.0984,"seconds":129},{"topic":"UNGA proceedings","share":0.0525,"seconds":69},{"topic":"Kyrgyzstan sovereignty","share":0.0522,"seconds":68},{"topic":"Climate finance","share":0.051,"seconds":67},{"topic":"UNSC veto power","share":0.0505,"seconds":66},{"topic":"US sanctions","share":0.0496,"seconds":65},{"topic":"UN treaty compliance","share":0.0493,"seconds":64},{"topic":"Kyrgyzstan UN role","share":0.0488,"seconds":64},{"topic":"UN operational mechanisms","share":0.0484,"seconds":63},{"topic":"UNSC reform","share":0.048,"seconds":63},{"topic":"US sanctions policy","share":0.0477,"seconds":62},{"topic":"Middle East conflict","share":0.0475,"seconds":62},{"topic":"UN sanctions registry","share":0.0466,"seconds":61}],"timeline":[[2,70,"UNGA proceedings"],[72,134,"Middle East conflict"],[135,199,"Kyrgyzstan UN role"],[200,260,"UNSC membership"],[262,325,"UNSC reform"],[326,397,"UNSC membership"],[398,459,"UN sanctions registry"],[460,524,"UN treaty compliance"],[526,592,"UNSC veto power"],[593,656,"UN operational mechanisms"],[657,722,"US sanctions"],[722,785,"US sanctions policy"],[786,848,"UN sanctions regime"],[849,916,"UN sanctions regime"],[918,991,"Kyrgyzstan climate policy"],[993,1055,"Kyrgyzstan climate policy"],[1057,1123,"Kyrgyzstan water resources"],[1124,1196,"Kyrgyzstan water resources"],[1197,1264,"Climate finance"],[1266,1334,"Kyrgyzstan sovereignty"]],"stances":{"UNGA proceedings":"Sadyr Zhaparov congratulates Mr. Rahman on his election as president of the 81st session and notes the upcoming 80th anniversary of the United Nations.","Middle East conflict":"Sadyr Zhaparov states that ongoing conflicts in Ukraine, Iran, Sudan, and Palestine demonstrate the complexity of the international environment and require global cooperation to strengthen peace.","Kyrgyzstan UN role":"Sadyr Zhaparov states that Kyrgyzstan has been elected to the UN Security Council and expresses gratitude for the support received.","UNSC membership":"Sadyr Zhaparov asserts that Kyrgyzstan views its Security Council membership as a responsibility and reaffirms the country's commitment to international law and multilateral cooperation.","UNSC reform":"Sadyr Zhaparov argues that expanding the UN Security Council membership is a necessary reform expected by the international community, specifically advocating for permanent seats for Africa and Latin America.","UN sanctions registry":"Sadyr Zhaparov proposes establishing a UN registry to verify and report on the implementation of adopted decisions, with administration by the UN Secretariat.","UN treaty compliance":"Sadyr Zhaparov outlines a four-stage process for assessing and addressing states that fail to implement United Nations decisions, including potential referral to the Security Council.","UNSC veto power":"Sadyr Zhaparov argues that permanent Security Council members should voluntarily refrain from using their veto power when it blocks the implementation of UN decisions.","UN operational mechanisms":"Sadyr Zhaparov proposes a new UN mechanism to treat organizational decisions as binding obligations and states Kyrgyzstan's readiness to lead discussions on it.","US sanctions":"Sadyr Zhaparov states that unjustified unilateral sanctions are used as an instrument of interference by individual countries and negatively affect small developing nations like Kyrgyzstan.","US sanctions policy":"Sadyr Zhaparov expresses appreciation for President Trump's decisions that he believes have strengthened state sovereignty and reduced interference by non-state actors, while advocating for UN-exclusive authority over sanctions.","UN sanctions regime":"Sadyr Zhaparov proposes establishing a unified UN mechanism to monitor and review sanctions to ensure transparency, prevent arbitrary use, and strengthen the UN's role in maintaining peace.","Kyrgyzstan climate policy":"Sadyr Zhaparov emphasizes the vulnerability of Kyrgyzstan to melting glaciers and calls for united international efforts to address climate challenges in mountainous regions.","Kyrgyzstan water resources":"Sadyr Zhaparov states that Kyrgyzstan is mobilizing domestic resources and implementing a state concept to preserve Lake Issyk-Kul amidst threats from glacier melt.","Climate finance":"Sadyr Zhaparov proposes a joint global partnership and pledges a financial contribution to preserve the climate as a shared legacy.","Kyrgyzstan sovereignty":"Sadyr Zhaparov states that Kyrgyzstan has chosen cooperation over confrontation and sustainable development since gaining independence."},"claims":[{"t":51,"quote":"congratulate Mr. Rahman on being elected as the president of the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly","topic":"UNGA proceedings","kind":"fact"},{"t":65,"quote":"last year the international community marked the 80th anniversary of the United Nations","topic":"UNGA proceedings","kind":"fact"},{"t":72,"quote":"For 80 years, this unique global organization has been standing at the forefront of safeguarding peace, security, and development.","topic":"Middle East conflict","kind":"fact"},{"t":95,"quote":"Iron conflicts and humanitarian crisis in different parts of the world including Ukraine, Iran, Sudan, and Palestine demonstrate the complexity of the current international environment","topic":"Middle East conflict","kind":"fact"},{"t":135,"quote":"In recent years, Central Asia has clearly demonstrated to the world its ability to resolve complex interstate issues peacefully.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan UN role","kind":"fact"},{"t":167,"quote":"for the first time in its history, Kyrgyzstan has been elected as a member of the United Nations Security Council for the 2027 2028 term.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan UN role","kind":"fact"},{"t":186,"quote":"The support of 142 member states not only confirms Kungasan's readiness to contribute to international affairs but also demonstrates the growing role of the Central Asian region in international relations.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan UN role","kind":"number"},{"t":200,"quote":"This achievement is clear evidence that even small states can make a meaningful contribution to addressing global challenges.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"fact"},{"t":218,"quote":"One year ago, standing at this stage, I started with confident that Kyrgyzstan is among those states whose experience enables them to put forward substantive issues for consideration by the Security Council.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"self_credit"},{"t":266,"quote":"since the establishment of the United Nations in 1945, its member states and leaders have attempted to undertake systematic Reforms","topic":"UNSC reform","kind":"fact"},{"t":317,"quote":"grant two permanent seats to the African continent with population of more than 1 billion people","topic":"UNSC reform","kind":"number"},{"t":329,"quote":"At the same time a number of developed countries have also expressed their aspiration to become members of the security council.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"fact"},{"t":351,"quote":"The systematic failure to implement the organization's decisions undermines the legitimacy of multilateralism concept creates the impression that international law is not binding and encourages disregard for international","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"fact"},{"t":398,"quote":"This registry could include the following information and ensure the implementation of such mechanisms as first systematically record and verify the level of implementation of adopted Second, establish the obligation for member states to regularly submit report in accordance with the agreed standards. Third, provide analytical uh reviews on sectors and regions. Fourth, ensure transparency and equal access to information for all states.","topic":"UN sanctions registry","kind":"fact"},{"t":434,"quote":"The registry could be administered by the United Nations Secretariat with the support of independent experts.","topic":"UN sanctions registry","kind":"fact"},{"t":460,"quote":"The second stage development of a monitoring mission to the country concerned to assess the objective obstacles preventing the implementation of the decision and to prepare recommendations.","topic":"UN treaty compliance","kind":"fact"},{"t":472,"quote":"The third stage consideration of the case right here at a plenary meeting of the general assembly to determine the status of the concerned state in terms of its systematic implementation or non-implementation of United Nations decisions.","topic":"UN treaty compliance","kind":"fact"},{"t":499,"quote":"In particular, the merit could be referred to the international to the United Nations Security Council for consideration of restrictive measures provide for the international law, including the first temporary restriction on participation in United Nations programs.","topic":"UN treaty compliance","kind":"fact"},{"t":530,"quote":"when cases of systematic non-implementation of United Nations decisions are considered, the permanent members of the security council should voluntarily refrain from using the veto.","topic":"UNSC veto power","kind":"fact"},{"t":639,"quote":"Starting from the 1st January 2027, as a member of the United Nations Security Council, we intend to formally submit all these proposals to the relevant Distinguished colleagues","topic":"UN operational mechanisms","kind":"fact"},{"t":660,"quote":"today's sanctions are in some cases used by individual countries as an instrument to interference to the international affairs of sovereign states.","topic":"US sanctions","kind":"fact"},{"t":670,"quote":"The negative consequences of unjustified unilateral sanctions also affect small countries such as ours which are at the stage of formation and development.","topic":"US sanctions","kind":"fact"},{"t":724,"quote":"interference by NOS's in the internal affairs and policies of the developing countries has practically seized","topic":"US sanctions policy","kind":"fact"},{"t":732,"quote":"President Trump's decisions have created conditions for strengthening the sovereignty of states","topic":"US sanctions policy","kind":"fact"},{"t":786,"quote":"The prior review of sanction would help ensure that their potential humanitarian, economic and social consequences are taken into account.","topic":"UN sanctions regime","kind":"fact"},{"t":817,"quote":"I believe it would be appropriate to establish under the opice of the United Nations the unified international mechanism for monitoring sanctions and reviewing them on a regular basis.","topic":"UN sanctions regime","kind":"self_credit"},{"t":902,"quote":"The average temperature of our planet and the number of climate related emergencies are rising rapidly.","topic":"UN sanctions regime","kind":"fact"},{"t":908,"quote":"where then a negative impact on energy, agriculture, food security and the economy as a whole has reached a significant scale.","topic":"UN sanctions regime","kind":"fact"},{"t":920,"quote":"We are particularly vulnerable to effect of melting glaciers and this is not an abstract threat.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","kind":"fact"},{"t":941,"quote":"In this regard, in 21st 23rd October 2027, Bishkak will host the second global mountain summit Bishkek plus 25.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","kind":"number"},{"t":967,"quote":"During my meeting with United Nations Secretary General Mr. Antonio Bishke on 31st August 2026, I proposed organizing this summit jointly with the These efforts demonstrate our country's leading role among mountain states and its commitment to unite international efforts to address their challenges.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","kind":"self_credit"},{"t":1042,"quote":"Over the past 170 years, the water level has fallen by almost 14 meters, while over the past five years, the rate of decline has reached approximately 7 cm per year.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","kind":"number"},{"t":1057,"quote":"101 rivers historically flowed into the lake, but today only 30 flow throughout the entire year.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","kind":"fact"},{"t":1069,"quote":"Over the past 70 years, more than 30% of the region mount mountain glaciers have melted.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","kind":"number"},{"t":1075,"quote":"Nearly 20% of this reduction has occurred within the just past 7 to 10 years.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","kind":"number"},{"t":1134,"quote":"The loss of Lake Isle would not be merely a local tragedy. It would become an irreversible environmental loss for the entire region.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","kind":"fact"},{"t":1159,"quote":"Please take a look at not so long ago, the RLC and Lake Chart just like today were full and reservoir was full of water. Today they have largely dried up.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","kind":"fact"},{"t":1202,"quote":"we are prepared to contribute 10 million US dollars from the state budget to this fund","topic":"Climate finance","kind":"number"},{"t":1207,"quote":"In our culture, there is a concept known as ammanat. It means the duty to carefully preserve what our ancestors have entrusted to us and to pass it on to future generations","topic":"Climate finance","kind":"fact"},{"t":1273,"quote":"This year, Kyrgystan celebrates the 31st anniversary of its independence.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan sovereignty","kind":"fact"},{"t":1282,"quote":"Since then, we chose cooperation over confrontation, creation over destruction, and embark upon the path of sustainable development.","topic":"Kyrgyzstan sovereignty","kind":"self_credit"}],"promises":[{"t":128,"quote":"Our country stands ready to serve as a mediator in addressing complex and contentious issues.","topic":"Middle East conflict","kind":"promise"},{"t":230,"quote":"We do not forget the promises we have made and will make every effort to implement them fully.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"promise"},{"t":255,"quote":"We intend to work closely with every country and remain committed to productive cooperation.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"promise"},{"t":374,"quote":"In this regard, we propose to develop the United Nations mechanism for the implementation of decisions and First, it is necessary to establish within the framework of the general assembly the permanent public registry of the implementation of resolutions and obligations by member states.","topic":"UNSC membership","kind":"promise"},{"t":584,"quote":"I propose establishing to open a working group with the general assembly with the participation of the interested member states.","topic":"UNSC veto power","kind":"promise"},{"t":645,"quote":"we intend to formally submit all these proposals to the relevant Distinguished colleagues","topic":"UN operational mechanisms","kind":"promise"},{"t":1202,"quote":"we are prepared to contribute 10 million US dollars from the state budget to this fund","topic":"Climate finance","kind":"promise"}],"attacks":[],"greece":[],"said":[],"pickup":[],"transcript":[{"t":2,"topic":"UNGA proceedings","text":"The assembly will hear an address by his Zadir Sapavov, president of the Kygis Republic. I request protocol to escort his excellency and invite him to address the Mr. President, Mr. Secretary General, distinguished heads of delegations, ladies and gentlemen, at the outset of my speech, I would like to sincerely congratulate Mr. Rahman on being elected as the president of the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly and wish him further success. As you are all aware, last year the international community marked the 80th anniversary of the United Nations."},{"t":72,"topic":"Middle East conflict","text":"For 80 years, this unique global organization has been standing at the forefront of safeguarding peace, security, and development. Although our organization brings together all countries of the world and serves as the principal platform for international cooperation, wars and tensions still persist in various regions. Iron conflicts and humanitarian crisis in different parts of the world including Ukraine, Iran, Sudan, and Palestine demonstrate the complexity of the current international environment and require all of us to work together to strengthen peace, security, and sustainable development. Kyrgyzstan is a country that believes in the power of dialogue and the peaceful settlement of conflicts. Our country stands ready to serve as a mediator in addressing complex and contentious issues."},{"t":135,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan UN role","text":"In recent years, Central Asia has clearly demonstrated to the world its ability to resolve complex interstate issues peacefully. Today, Central Asia is becoming an example of constructive interstate cooperation as well as economic, infrastructural, and technological In this regard, we are ready to share the experience we have accumulated along this path. Distinguished heads of delegations, for the first time in its history, Kyrgyzstan has been elected as a member of the United Nations Security Council for the 2027 2028 term. Taking this opportunity, I would like to express my profound gratitude to all countries that supported our candidacy. The support of 142 member states not only confirms Kungasan's readiness to contribute to international affairs but also demonstrates the growing role of the Central Asian region in international relations."},{"t":200,"topic":"UNSC membership","text":"This achievement is clear evidence that even small states can make a meaningful contribution to addressing global challenges. We fully understand that membership in the security council is not a privilege or award. It is a great responsibility. One year ago, standing at this stage, I started with confident that Kyrgyzstan is among those states whose experience enables them to put forward substantive issues for consideration by the Security Council. We do not forget the promises we have made and will make every effort to implement them fully. Therefore, I would like to reiterate Kyrgystan firmly adheres to international law, justice, and the protection of human rights and supports the multilateral system based on mutual respect, balance of interest, and responsibility toward future We intend to work closely with every country and remain committed to productive cooperation."},{"t":262,"topic":"UNSC reform","text":"Distinguished colleagues, as you all may know, since the establishment of the United Nations in 1945, its member states and leaders have attempted to undertake systematic Reforms have ranged from expanding the membership of the security council to modernizing mechanisms of governance, peacekeeping, and the protection of human rights. These initiatives continue to be actively discussed at the international However, despite numerous efforts and years of discussion, the institutional structure of our organization continues to face various challenges. Therefore, I believe that expanding the membership of the security council is one of the reforms that the international community expects. I consider it fair to grant two permanent seats to the African continent with population of more than 1 billion people and one permanent seat to Latin Latin America."},{"t":326,"topic":"UNSC membership","text":"Today the world has changed. At the same time a number of developed countries have also expressed their aspiration to become members of the security council. I believe that the request of these countries should be also considered. Another issue in my view the weakness of effective mechanisms for ensuring the implementation of the decisions of the The systematic failure to implement the organization's decisions undermines the legitimacy of multilateralism concept creates the impression that international law is not binding and encourages disregard for international Therefore, we must expand and strengthen the authority of the United Nations. In this regard, we propose to develop the United Nations mechanism for the implementation of decisions and First, it is necessary to establish within the framework of the general assembly the permanent public registry of the implementation of resolutions and obligations by member states."},{"t":398,"topic":"UN sanctions registry","text":"This registry could include the following information and ensure the implementation of such mechanisms as first systematically record and verify the level of implementation of adopted Second, establish the obligation for member states to regularly submit report in accordance with the agreed standards. Third, provide analytical uh reviews on sectors and regions. Fourth, ensure transparency and equal access to information for all states. The registry could be administered by the United Nations Secretariat with the support of independent experts. If decisions of the organization are systematically not implemented, I propose the gradual response mechanisms. The first stage, an official notification to the concerned state followed by consultations with the relevant bodies of the organization."},{"t":460,"topic":"UN treaty compliance","text":"The second stage development of a monitoring mission to the country concerned to assess the objective obstacles preventing the implementation of the decision and to prepare recommendations. The third stage consideration of the case right here at a plenary meeting of the general assembly to determine the status of the concerned state in terms of its systematic implementation or non-implementation of United Nations decisions. The fourth stage, the initiation of appropriate procedures under the charter of the United Nations with regard to states that fail to comply with the requirements. In particular, the merit could be referred to the international to the United Nations Security Council for consideration of restrictive measures provide for the international law, including the first temporary restriction on participation in United Nations programs. The second recommendations to review existing formats of cooperation and other diplomatic measures where appropriate."},{"t":526,"topic":"UNSC veto power","text":"Distinguished colleagues, I believe that when cases of systematic non-implementation of United Nations decisions are considered, the permanent members of the security council should voluntarily refrain from using the veto. If a mechanism designed to ensure the implementation of the organization's decisions is systematically blocked by permanent members through the use of the veto, such practice would no longer be consistent with the spirit of the UN charter and the principle of equal responsibility of member states. Therefore, we should once again consider the possibility of voluntarily restraining to use the veto in matters related to the implementation of the organization's decisions. This approach could promote universality, the um equality of all states regardless of their size, the primacy of diplomacy and avoid the double standards. In order to implement this initiative, I propose establishing to open a working group with the general assembly with the participation of the interested member states."},{"t":593,"topic":"UN operational mechanisms","text":"The working group could conduct intergovernmental consultations and prepare a report on possible models for the proposed mechanism. Following these consultations, the general assembly and the security council should consider adopting the relevant resolutions and decisions. The strategic objective of this initiative is to ensure that the decisions of the organization are treated not merely as political recommendations but as genuine international obligations. Improvement of their implementation would strengthen the rule of international law and reinforce confidence in the United Nations. Kyrgyzstan is ready to initiate interstate discussions on the development of such a mechanism. Starting from the 1st January 2027, as a member of the United Nations Security Council, we intend to formally submit all these proposals to the relevant Distinguished colleagues, I would like to address separately uh the issues of sanctions."},{"t":657,"topic":"US sanctions","text":"As we have all observed today's sanctions are in some cases used by individual countries as an instrument to interference to the international affairs of sovereign states. The negative consequences of unjustified unilateral sanctions also affect small countries such as ours which are at the stage of formation and development. In some cases, such practices led to interference into the internal affairs of independent states and contradict to the fundamental principles of international law. Some major states in pursuit of their own interest put pressure on smaller countries such as Kyrgyzstan attempting to force them to bargain over certain matters and issuing In these contexts, I would like to take this opportunity tonight to note that the decision made by the president of the United States, his excellency Mr. Donald Trump, after taking office to hold funding of non-governmental organizations around the world."},{"t":722,"topic":"US sanctions policy","text":"As a result, interference by NOS's in the internal affairs and policies of the developing countries has practically seized. President Trump's decisions have created conditions for strengthening the sovereignty of states and enabling them to independently choose their own path of development. Therefore, taking this opportunity, I would like to express my appreciation to Mr. Trump for ending the founding of the NOS's. Distinguished colleagues, decisions to impose sanctions should be taken in accordance with the UN charter and the exclusively within the mandate of the security council. We believe that the mandate to impose sanctions against the state should belong exclusively to the United Nations. In my view, this is the only possible way to improve the international sanctions system. First and foremost, we should introduce a mandatory mechanisms under which all sanction and measures are reviewed in advance by organization."},{"t":786,"topic":"UN sanctions regime","text":"The prior review of sanction would help ensure that their potential humanitarian, economic and social consequences are taken into account. An important element of the proposed mechanism should be the enhanced transparency and States initiating sanctions should clearly indicate the legal basis for such measures, explain their necessity and objectives and define clear criteria and conditions for their eventual removal. I believe it would be appropriate to establish under the opice of the United Nations the unified international mechanism for monitoring sanctions and reviewing them on a regular basis. Only then can the central role of the United Nations in maintaining international peace and security be strengthened and its mandate This would also help eliminate the risk the sanctions being used arbitrarily by one country against another for political or economic gain."},{"t":849,"topic":"UN sanctions regime","text":"No state should act arbitrarily. The United Nations must be strong. Every country should tremble than the United Nations intends to impose sanctions. Only then the strict international order can be established in the world. Otherwise, some decisions of the United Nations may no longer be taken seriously while states simply act according to their own discretion. Every decision of the United Nations should be implemented without delay. If a state fails to implement such decisions and other countries seize cooperation with it, the effectiveness of sanctions will increase only then where decision will be implemented promptly and the United Nations will become a strong and respected organization. Otherwise, existing sanction will become sort of children's toy. Dear friends, as you know, climate change has become a global challenge. The average temperature of our planet and the number of climate related emergencies are rising rapidly. where then a negative impact on energy, agriculture, food security and the economy as a whole has reached a significant scale."},{"t":918,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","text":"Kyrgyzstan is a mountainous country. We are particularly vulnerable to effect of melting glaciers and this is not an abstract threat. It is an environmental challenge of the regional significance. I believe it is extremely important and necessary to unite international efforts in addressing climate challenges and the problems facing mountainous regions. In this regard, in 21st 23rd October 2027, Bishkak will host the second global mountain summit Bishkek plus 25. Taking this opportunity, I invite all of you to participate in the summit. The summit will serve as the concluding event for the five years of action for the development of mountain regions for 2023 2027 term. During my meeting with United Nations Secretary General Mr. Antonio Bishke on 31st August 2026, I proposed organizing this summit jointly with the These efforts demonstrate our country's leading role among mountain states and its commitment to unite international efforts to address their challenges."},{"t":993,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan climate policy","text":"Therefore, Kyrgyzstan stands fully ready to serve with the United Nations as the voice of small and developing countries in addressing the climate related challenges affecting mountainous regions. Distinguished heads of delegations, continuing the topic of climate change, I would like to draw your attention to another environmental challenge. As we all know, global problems and disasters often have their origins at the local level. The issue concerns the unique high altitude lake is which is the part of the UNESCO biosphere reserve and is included among wetlands of international importance under the Ramsar framework. Today this invaluable natural water reservoir is under severe climatic stress. Over the past 170 years, the water level has fallen by almost 14 meters, while over the past five years, the rate of decline has reached approximately 7 cm per year."},{"t":1057,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","text":"101 rivers historically flowed into the lake, but today only 30 flow throughout the entire year. The main source of water for Lake Isul is the glacier of the Tianan mountains. Over the past 70 years, more than 30% of the region mount mountain glaciers have melted. Nearly 20% of this reduction has occurred within the just past 7 to 10 years. This process poses a direct threat to the biodiversity of the region and economic stability of hundreds of thousands of people. As part of our efforts to preserve Lake Isul, Kyrgystan is mobilizing all available domestic resources. In particular, the state concept for the sustainable development of the SQL ecological and economic system until 2030 is being implemented. Strict environmental restrictions have been introduced and pilot projects have been launched to monitor the depths of the lake using the underwater robotic technologies. Large-scale efforts to clean the lake bed and its waters are continuing on a regular b basis."},{"t":1124,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan water resources","text":"The scale and speed of climate change exceed the capacity of any individual mountain country and require consolidated international action. The loss of Lake Isle would not be merely a local tragedy. It would become an irreversible environmental loss for the entire region. In order to understand the potential scale of this environmental challenge and the cost association with preventing, it is important to look at the fate of the RLC and lake chart, both of which have suffered severely over recent decades. Please take a look at not so long ago, the RLC and Lake Chart just like today were full and reservoir was full of water. Today they have largely dried up. For us, this example must serve as a clear warning. Taking this opportunity, I officially call upon the United Nations International Financial Institutions and donor countries to support our initiative to establish under the opice of the organization the special joint trust fund for the preservation of Lake Isle."},{"t":1197,"topic":"Climate finance","text":"In order to demonstrate our commitments through concrete actions, we are prepared to contribute 10 million US dollars from the state budget to this fund. In our culture, there is a concept known as ammanat. It means the duty to carefully preserve what our ancestors have entrusted to us and to pass it on to future generations. In this sense, it would be fair to state that legacy is our shared climate ammon. We are not asking for help. We are proposing a joint global partnership to preserve this unique natural resource for the earth. Before it is too late, let us unite our efforts and resources within the framework of this trust fund. Distinguished participants, ladies and gentlemen, the challenges before us are shared. Therefore, our response to them must be also collective. We stand at the threshold of a new era of change. safe, just and sustainable future can be built only through collective action."},{"t":1266,"topic":"Kyrgyzstan sovereignty","text":"The destiny of our planet and future generations is the responsibility of each and every one of us. This year, Kyrgystan celebrates the 31st anniversary of its independence. Throughout this period, our country has faced many different challenges. Since then, we chose cooperation over confrontation, creation over destruction, and embark upon the path of sustainable development. There is one thing we must all remember. Peace does not come about by itself. Therefore, let us turn our words into action, concrete steps that improve people's lives and protect nature. Let us build together a world which dialect is stronger than weapons. Mutual respect is stronger than fear and cooperation among nations delivers tangible results for all Thank you very much for your attention. On behalf of the assembly, I wish to thank the president of the Kirgis"}],"compare":[{"topic":"UN & its reform","share":0.4934,"mean":0.2032,"rank":9,"others":170},{"topic":"Sanctions","share":0.0973,"mean":0.0026,"rank":3,"others":5},{"topic":"Kyrgyzstan","share":0.0522,"mean":0.0003,"rank":2,"others":1},{"topic":"Climate Change","share":0.051,"mean":0.0428,"rank":68,"others":77},{"topic":"Middle East","share":0.0475,"mean":0.0071,"rank":18,"others":19}]};
